From: Yankı Küçük <kendinikertenkelebek@me.com>
Subject: [PATCH] syscall: add aarch64 support

catbox only built on x86: the dispatch table referenced system calls that
newer architectures do not have (open, creat, mkdir, rename, ...) and the
tracer read and changed registers through PTRACE_PEEKUSER, PTRACE_GETREGS
and PTRACE_SETREGS with x86 register names.

- Guard every legacy entry of the dispatch table with its __NR_ macro, so
  the table builds on architectures that only have the *at variants.
- Add renameat2, fchmodat2 and openat2 (flags read from struct open_how)
  when the headers define them. The C library on aarch64 routes rename()
  through renameat2, and none of the three was checked before on any
  architecture.
- Treat mkdirat like mkdir (EEXIST for parents of allowed paths) where no
  mkdir system call exists, since mkdir() is implemented with mkdirat there.
- Read system call arguments through PEEK_ARG(). On x86_64 and i386 it
  expands to the same PTRACE_PEEKUSER call as before. On aarch64 it reads
  the NT_PRSTATUS register set (x0..x5 arguments, x8 call number).
- On aarch64 a denied call is skipped by setting NT_ARM_SYSTEM_CALL to -1
  on syscall entry, and x0 is set to the error code on syscall exit.
- Fail the build with #error on architectures that have no register
  layout, instead of silently using the x86_64 one.

The x86_64 and i386 code paths are unchanged apart from the three new
table entries.
---
diff --git a/src/syscall.c b/src/syscall.c
index 36cdfbd..37add0f 100644
--- a/src/syscall.c
+++ b/src/syscall.c
@@ -19,6 +19,11 @@
 #include <sys/un.h>
 #include <sys/socket.h>
 #include <netdb.h>
